1.	Design a payroll management system for an organization with different types of employees.
Create an abstract class Employee with private fields id (int), name (String), and department (String). Include a constructor, getters, and an abstract method double calculateSalary().
Create two subclasses:
FullTimeEmployee: Adds baseSalary (double) and bonus (double). calculateSalary() returns baseSalary + bonus.
ContractEmployee: Adds hourlyRate (double) and hoursWorked (int). calculateSalary() returns hourlyRate * hoursWorked.
Create a PayrollManager class that maintains a List<Employee> and implements the following methods using Java Streams and Lambdas:
Map<String, Double> getDepartmentWiseTotalSalary(): Returns a map of each department to its total salary payout.
List<String> getTopEarners(double threshold): Returns a sorted list (alphabetical) of employee names whose calculated salary is strictly greater than the threshold.
Constraints
1 ≤ Number of employees ≤ 10⁴
Salaries and rates are non-negative doubles.
Sample Input & Output
Input:
FullTimeEmployee(101, "Alice", "IT", 60000, 10000) -> Salary: 70000.0
ContractEmployee(102, "Bob", "IT", 500, 100) -> Salary: 50000.0
FullTimeEmployee(103, "Charlie", "HR", 55000, 5000) -> Salary: 60000.0
threshold = 55000
Output:
Department-wise Total Salary: {IT=120000.0, HR=60000.0}
Top Earners (> 55000): ["Alice", "Charlie"

3.   Contest Leaderboard Ranking
  An online judge evaluates submissions and ranks participants at the end of a contest.
  Each participant is represented by a Participant object containing the following attributes:
  	String username: The unique handle of the participant.
  	int problemsSolved: The total number of problems successfully solved.
  	int penaltyTime: The total penalty time accumulated (in minutes).
  	int rating: The participant's current competitive rating.
  At the end of the contest, you are given a list of N participants. Your task is to sort and rank all participants according to the official contest tie-breaking rules:
  1.	Problems Solved: Participants with a higher problemsSolved count rank higher (descending order).
  2.	Penalty Time: If two or more participants have solved the same number of problems, the participant with a lower penaltyTime ranks higher (ascending order).
  3.	Current Rating: If both problemsSolved and penaltyTime are identical, the participant with a higher rating ranks higher (descending order).
  4.	Username: If all three metrics above are tied, participants are ranked alphabetically by username in lexicographical order (ascending order, case-sensitive).
  Input Format
  •	The first line contains an integer N, representing the total number of participants.
  •	Each of the next N lines contains a string and three space-separated integers: username, problemsSolved, penaltyTime, and rating.
  Output Format
  •	Print N lines representing the final leaderboard from Rank 1 to Rank N.
  •	Each line should display the username, problemsSolved, penaltyTime, and rating separated by a single space.
  Constraints
  •	1 ≤ N ≤ 10⁵
  •	1 ≤ |username| ≤ 20 (contains only alphanumeric characters and underscores)
  •	0 ≤ problemsSolved ≤ 20
  •	0 ≤ penaltyTime ≤ 10⁶
  •	0 ≤ rating ≤ 4000
  •	All usernames are guaranteed to be unique.
  Test case 1:
  Input 
  5
  alice 5 120 1850
  bob 6 210 1700
  charlie 5 120 1920
  dave 5 150 2000
  eve 5 120 1920
        Output 1
  bob 6 210 1700
  charlie 5 120 1920
  eve 5 120 1920
  alice 5 120 1850
  dave 5 150 2000
